NAME
    cp - copy a file

SYNOPSIS
    cp [-f] <src> <dst>

DESCRIPTION
    Copies the file <src> to <dst>. Streams the data, so any size copies.
    Paths resolve against the current directory.

    An existing <dst> is REFUSED:

        cp: /B.TXT exists (use -f to overwrite)

    -f overwrites it. This differs from POSIX cp, which overwrites silently;
    the choice is deliberate on a system with no undo, no trash and no
    snapshots, where the contents of the overwritten file cannot be recovered.
    mv behaves the same way.

    It is a refusal and not a prompt, on purpose. Asking needs the keyboard,
    and cp has none when it runs as a pipeline stage or under cpu on another
    machine - so a prompt would work in the interactive case and hang or fail
    everywhere else. A refusal behaves identically everywhere.

    Only -f is accepted, and only before the operands. Any other -word is an
    error rather than a filename: for a command that destroys data, treating a
    mistyped flag as the source is the wrong way to be forgiving.

SEE ALSO
    mv, cat, write
